} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106945"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e4a5a49c-1d87-5a55-b8a5-ab4db05c8306" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "4f5fa2bf-f429-5b6a-9c23-83970c257a40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "d8423897-772a-5ab5-a0b6-f859ce44de59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "dec01ba0-aeec-5fcb-beae-7176e0184b80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "2cee80e0-95a5-5db9-ad0a-e0bb7c198b70" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4843aca-9f17-515a-989d-db53723de0df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107314/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-107314/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-107314/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-107314/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-107314/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-107314/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107314" :question/id #uuid "43d63a39-99ac-5de8-969c-b2b73de1a528" :question/math-academy-id "q-107314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-107314/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107314"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9a92bc77-efd3-5ccb-9e80-466e144a3a58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "a7d15386-9280-58fc-8cf6-8e8b8ea50290" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "cc606a5d-a2f8-5e2a-9047-41a81815b986" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e1540114-d336-588e-b5b1-a70cccf42475" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "e99e7e7f-1ade-5fba-80f5-ca53e99d12aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f96961ad-100f-5975-97ce-574a82ece562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75730/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-75730/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-75730/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-75730/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-75730/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-75730/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75730" :question/id #uuid "a582afda-86b0-5457-9d06-6c4604568827" :question/math-academy-id "q-75730" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-75730/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75730"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3b0b9e07-f029-5bdf-8a52-3f5fa8798cb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "5f0aee3a-36e5-5b4c-86d9-7245db95edbc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "88d3d43b-7e5e-5960-8512-650afbd429ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b670d7bf-02b1-5311-89ce-c85f33a09e8a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2721dd86-c2ae-50f7-8282-f7e7cb0c5394" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1e310c-86ec-50a8-97d0-bf0935117919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75729/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-75729/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-75729/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-75729/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-75729/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-75729/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75729" :question/id #uuid "d7a91e6f-ec17-5b19-a508-d46535ff5b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75729" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-75729/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75729"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ea35c061-fa8f-516c-83c2-fd5cfffffec9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "ae9baf22-6b0f-5a88-848f-e8fb9c700e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "016fd12e-792a-527b-9b0d-edbc565722ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "f766fc58-1564-5cea-adf0-0cb51669a64d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "93eb26c5-0b3d-5382-899e-93963acebaa2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd2bf80e-f9fd-5288-89f6-16356c5fb6fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-108044/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-108044/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-108044/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-108044/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-108044/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-108044/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-108044" :question/id #uuid "d6270985-6d2a-5318-afb3-6b0266d6b180" :question/math-academy-id "q-108044" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-108044/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-108044"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5476"] :question/problem "Determine if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/82b99a6e1a271ac0b205a3199b023a582877692c3a61dd4b7144312342fea761.png) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/2b9e07d0a0089107dc34bb092167719b6e83a4c41ee9f055949a43549449d65e.png) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5891"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/9f9c7bc79c4e294e45f34d9e10c0e429c33ce95730d13aaaf337f7a9cb22f1a3.png) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5478"] :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/f5ac973d5d7dd390f8cf9231515815f334d0c01dafeab14ba58fef25db4a8e8d.png) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/a7e303f19be04db829faaa41133c70d7b6e5647ed75063674ca564a2952c09eb.png) Determine the value of $c.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5477"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/f35353367a395e7b3560aa6f7ef0da0936cefbced9e76d6d7d8f91426a7a1c9b.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/0b4f33107845bf52d80802844bc6faaec6ab9f4bd159a87838497d2f6abe72d1.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/ea0bc4d487e57434694dac1835e082f63c9467b496933c8e36770334e77713f5.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/c87d5c13bbd73d56f44af3ec1b4a867c86e8bf4f709fa869ab8d142424cbe8c4.png) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
